 09_4	History shows the lean. In the apportionment after the eighteen thirty census, made with Jefferson's rule, New York's exact share was thirty eight point five nine three seats. Peyton Young's table records that it received forty.
 09_5	So the two families trade flaws. Hamilton's rule keeps the quota promise but can take a seat back. Jefferson's rule never takes a seat back but can break the quota promise.
 10_1	Jefferson rounds every quotient down. Change where the rounding happens and you get the rest of the family, each one built the same way, with a sliding yardstick and one list of signposts.
-10_2	John Quincy Adams proposed rounding up instead. Now any piece of a share earns a whole seat, so the lean flips toward small towns. At fifteen seats Adams gives two, four and nine. Cedar falls below its share of exactly ten.
+10_2	John Quincy Adams proposed rounding up instead. Now any piece of a quotient earns a whole seat, so the lean flips toward small towns. At fifteen seats Adams gives two, four and nine. Cedar falls below its share of exactly ten.
 10_3	Between always down and always up sits the compromise, round to the nearest. What does that give at fifteen seats?
 10_4	Two, three, ten, the same as Hamilton. This is Daniel Webster's rule. At sixteen seats it gives two, four, ten, and at seventeen, two, four, eleven. Nobody ever loses a seat. In our three towns, Webster does what we hoped Hamilton would do.
 10_5	One more relative is worth knowing, because it became the law for the United States House in nineteen forty one. It is called the Huntington Hill method. Between one and two, it rounds up once a quotient passes the square root of one times two, about one point four one. For our towns it agrees with Webster at fifteen, sixteen and seventeen seats.
@@ -85,8 +85,8 @@
 14_4	Neither. Suppose the city gets nine. Then the last seat goes to one village. Call it the fifth. Now let only the fifth village grow, to two hundred twenty two people. The city's share falls to six point nine nine, so it can have at most seven seats. The fifth village's share is one point eight eight, so it can have at most two. That leaves at least one seat for the other three villages.
 14_5	So some village with an unchanged population goes from no seats to one, while the city, also unchanged, drops from nine seats to seven or fewer. The city did not shrink, and it lost seats to a village that did not grow. The population promise is broken.
 14_6	Suppose instead the city gets eight. Then two villages get one seat each. Call them the fourth and fifth. This time shrink the other two villages to a single person each. The city's share rises to nine point zero two, so it needs at least nine seats. Only one seat is left for the fourth and fifth villages, so one of them drops to zero. That village did not shrink, and it lost its seat to a city that did not grow.
-14_7	Both choices fail. So no rule that keeps the quota promise can keep the population promise for every map of five towns, and the same trick works with more. With three towns, Webster's rule keeps both. With four, as of that paper, nobody knew.
-14_8	The same paper shows a way out. If the rule may draw lots, it can keep the quota promise, never take a seat back, and give every town exactly its share on average.
+14_7	Both choices fail. So no rule that keeps the quota promise can keep the population promise for every map of five towns, and the same trick works with more. With three towns, Webster's rule keeps both. With four, without the extra assumption, nobody knew as of that paper.
+14_8	The same paper shows what a lottery can add. If the rule may draw lots, it can keep the quota promise, never take a seat back, and give every town exactly its share on average. But even a lottery cannot keep the quota promise and the population promise together.
 15_1	Here is the whole map. Hamilton's rule keeps the quota promise, but it can take a seat back when the house grows, and it can punish a town for growing.
 15_2	Jefferson, Adams, Webster and Hill never take a seat back and never punish growth, but each can break the quota promise. The quota method of Balinski and Young keeps the quota promise and never takes a seat back, and the theorem says it must break the population promise for some map.
 15_3	Here is one to try yourself. Under Webster's rule, suppose Cedar grows while Ash and Birch stay exactly the same, and the council stays at fifteen seats. Can Ash ever gain a seat?
